+# Export duplicate-effect hunt
+
+Checked 2026-10-24 at revision `d6dc296300924c6acb3ee01c4d58e9c8518576f7`.
+Scope: `Exports.apply` in `app/exports.py`, interruption and sequential retries
+of the same request ID and payload. Stopping condition: check this operation's
+duplicate-effect risk and its direct recovery paths. No product repair or claim
+verification was requested or performed.
+
+## Demonstrated defect: committed item without receipt duplicates on retry
+
+The supported behavior in `app/export-contract.md` requires exactly one item
+per accepted request, including retries after a durable effect before a response.
+`Exports.apply` checks `receipts` (line 15), inserts an item (line 18), explicitly
+commits it (line 19), invokes `after_effect` (lines 20–21), and only then inserts
+the receipt (line 22). The outer connection context does not undo an already
+committed transaction.
+
+Reachable trace on an initially empty local SQLite file:
+
+1. Call `apply("request-1", "payload", after_effect=...)`.
+2. Item insertion commits; the hook raises or terminates the process before
+   receipt insertion. One durable item remains and no receipt exists.
+3. Recover and retry the same ID and payload. The receipt check misses, so a
+   second item commits and a receipt is recorded. The retry returns `True`.
+4. Further retries leave the two items in place.
+
+Harm: one logical request produces two persistent exported items. Repeating the
+interruption before receipt insertion can repeat the same mechanism further
+(code inspection; the executed schedules each interrupt once).
+
+Fix direction: make the item insertion and receipt insertion one SQLite
+transaction. Because both are local SQLite writes, they can commit atomically.
+The isolated control below supports this direction under the supported sequential
+deployment; it is not a product patch or a broader correctness proof.
+
+## Executed evidence and coverage
+
+Run from the repository root: `python3 tests/hunt_witness.py`.
+The witness calls the real `Exports` entry point, uses fresh temporary databases,
+and checks the observable item list. It exits 1 on the current implementation:
+
+| Schedule | Observed final items | Result |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Successful call, close/reopen, two same-ID retries | `['payload']` | Pass |
+| Hook exception, retry on same connection, close/reopen and retry | `['payload', 'payload']` | Fail |
+| Hook exception, close/reopen, retry | `['payload', 'payload']` | Fail |
+| Child process exits via `os._exit(73)` in hook, parent reopens and retries | `['payload', 'payload']` | Fail |
+
+The termination child exited 73 as required, establishing that the tested hook
+ran. Each final recovery calls `apply` twice to also exercise receipt suppression
+after a successful retry. The witness exits zero only if all four schedules
+produce exactly one item; unexpected execution errors remain nonzero.
+
+Control executed in a temporary copy of `app/exports.py` and the saved witness:
+remove only the explicit `self.db.commit()` line, then run that copied witness.
+All four schedules passed, exit 0. The unchanged product is the concrete broken
+case rejected by the oracle; the temporary atomic variant confirms the witness
+can also pass when this failure mechanism is removed. This is a directed test,
+not generated search or formal verification. Python 3.9.6, SQLite 3.43.2.
+
+## Assumptions, discovery, and limits
+
+Applied the contract's fault model: one sequential worker, restart after the
+previous worker stops, committed SQLite state survives, eventual recovery and
+repeated calls after transient failure, identical payload for same-ID retries.
+No concurrency or overlapping requests were needed. The hook exception and real
+process termination exercise the explicitly supported effect/receipt boundary.
+
+Read the root governing records and searched code/records for `Exports`,
+`receipts`, `after_effect`, export-contract references and single-writer links.
+The export implementation has no other callers or recovery loop in this fixture;
+the witness supplies the repeated calls promised by the contract. The local-only
+gate is respected by temporary local SQLite files. The note-payload and import
+requirements govern separate interfaces. `CLAIM-single-writer` concerns files
+under `data/`; this hunt uses isolated temporary databases and does not verify or
+change that claim or its evidence. No nested governing records were found.
+
+Not tested: every SQL instruction boundary, transient I/O error injection,
+machine power loss, storage corruption/loss, changed-payload retries, concurrent
+workers, or unrelated harms such as abandoned work and false status. Concurrency,
+overlap and storage loss/corruption are explicitly unsupported; other untested
+conditions remain coverage gaps. No separate conditional or model-only findings
+are asserted. Stopped after establishing this operation's duplicate-effect risk.
+
+Product code, governing records, and existing evidence are preserved. Added only
+this report and `tests/hunt_witness.py`; all witness/control databases and copied
+product files are temporary and removed after execution.
